# smart-parking-system-
smart parking system iot based project with the help of blynk app
This code will:
Connect the ESP8266 to your Wi-Fi network.
Connect to the Blynk cloud service.
Read the IR sensor to detect if a parking spot is occupied or empty.
Update the status ("Occupied" / "Empty") on the Blynk app using a Virtual Pin (V0).
Control a servo motor (simulating a gate/barrier) via a button on the Blynk app (Virtual Pin V1).
Send a push notification alert to the Blynk app when the parking spot becomes empty.
Display the status visually using an LED widget on Blynk (Virtual Pin V2).
Assumptions:
You are using an ESP8266-based board (like NodeMCU, Wemos D1 Mini). If using ESP32, library includes might change slightly (ESP32WiFi.h, WiFi.h, BlynkSimpleEsp32.h).
The IR sensor outputs LOW when an object (car) is detected and HIGH when it's clear (empty). Adjust IR_DETECTED_STATE if your sensor behaves differently.
The servo represents a gate: 0 degrees = Closed, 90 degrees = Open. Adjust GATE_CLOSED_ANGLE and GATE_OPEN_ANGLE if needed.
1. Prerequisites:
Hardware:
ESP8266 Board (NodeMCU, Wemos D1 Mini, etc.)
Servo Motor (e.g., SG90)
IR Obstacle Avoidance Sensor
Jumper Wires
Breadboard (optional)
Power Supply (USB or external 5V for servo if needed)
Software:
Arduino IDE installed.
ESP8266 Board support installed in Arduino IDE (Boards Manager).
Blynk Library: Install via Arduino IDE Library Manager (Search for "Blynk").
Servo Library: Usually included with Arduino IDE (Servo.h).
Blynk App:
Install Blynk (legacy or Blynk IoT 2.0) on your phone.
Create a new project.
Get the Blynk Auth Token (emailed to you when creating the project).
Add the following Widgets to your project dashboard:
Labeled Value Display (Set to Virtual Pin V0, Label: Parking Status, Reading Rate: PUSH).
Button (Set to Virtual Pin V1, Mode: Switch, Label: Gate Control, ON Value: 1, OFF Value: 0).
LED (Set to Virtual Pin V2, Label: Spot Status).
2. Wiring:
IR Sensor:
VCC -> 3.3V or 5V on ESP8266 (check sensor specs)
GND -> GND on ESP8266
OUT -> D2 on ESP8266 (or any other digital pin)
Servo Motor:
VCC -> VIN (5V) on ESP8266 (Important: If servo draws much current, power it from a separate stable 5V source, connecting its GND to ESP8266 GND).
GND -> GND on ESP8266
Signal -> D1 on ESP8266 (or any other PWM-capable pin
